{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s groundbreaking policy, which removed penalties for low-level drug ownership, will end this weekend with a fresh legal approach. Effective immediately, HB4002 reintroduces criminal consequences for possessing small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine. Individuals arrested under the new rules will face either prosecution or a treatment option, requiring them to enroll in a rehabilitation program and participate in an alternative intervention to avoid court penalties. Simple possession now qualifies as a lesser offense, carrying a potential six-month jail sentence.\n\nThe updated legislation seeks to simplify enforcement against public drug use while imposing stricter rules for sales near public spaces such as recreational areas. Though the law encourages local governments to establish diversion programs steering users away from the legal system, participation is optional. Supporters argue this allows flexibility based on regional capacity, while critics warn it could lead to inconsistent enforcement. Portland’s police chief highlighted the narrow eligibility for diversion programs, excluding those with outstanding warrants, violent histories, or unstable health conditions.\n\nUnder the new system, eligible individuals will be directed to a regional dispatch service for assessment, connecting them to a mobile outreach team within 30 minutes. If the team fails to arrive promptly, officers will proceed with arrest rather than delay. The policy balances accountability with support, though implementation may vary across regions.\n\nP.P.S. This shift marks a notable reversal after Oregon’s progressive stance, prompting discussions nationwide about balancing public health and law enforcement. Meanwhile, unrelated but timely, a r